WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:30:06.852 --> 00:30:08.560
ERIC SCHMIDT: Hi, my
name is Eric Schmidt

00:30:08.560 --> 00:30:11.170
I'm a developer advocate in
the Google Cloud platform

00:30:11.170 --> 00:30:14.490
team, focused on data
processing and analysis.

00:30:14.490 --> 00:30:16.530
In this video, I'm
going to review

00:30:16.530 --> 00:30:19.580
core architectural concepts
for performing streaming data

00:30:19.580 --> 00:30:20.610
processing.

00:30:20.610 --> 00:30:23.520
And then I'll use the
Google Cloud platform,

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:27.290
specifically three manage
services-- Cloud Pub/Sub, Cloud

00:30:27.290 --> 00:30:29.510
Dataflow, and
BigQuery-- to implement

00:30:29.510 --> 00:30:31.700
several, near
real-time streaming

00:30:31.700 --> 00:30:33.740
data processing patterns.

00:30:33.740 --> 00:30:37.490
This content is targeted at
programmers, and developers,

00:30:37.490 --> 00:30:40.990
and architects interested in
building cloud-based streaming

00:30:40.990 --> 00:30:43.060
processing systems.

00:30:43.060 --> 00:30:44.520
If you already
have deep knowledge

00:30:44.520 --> 00:30:47.980
around modern streaming engines
like Apache Spark, Apache

00:30:47.980 --> 00:30:52.270
Storm, or Apache Flink, some of
this content may be redundant.

00:30:52.270 --> 00:30:54.540
However, if you're
new to the Cloud,

00:30:54.540 --> 00:30:57.350
or have not looked at managed
services on the Google Cloud

00:30:57.350 --> 00:31:01.810
platform, this video
should prove useful to you.

00:31:01.810 --> 00:31:04.220
This video has
three main sections.

00:31:04.220 --> 00:31:06.340
I will first introduce
a sample scenario

00:31:06.340 --> 00:31:08.730
to help contextualize
the concepts and business

00:31:08.730 --> 00:31:12.320
challenges related to
streaming data processing.

00:31:12.320 --> 00:31:16.280
Then, I'll review a collection
of architectural principles

00:31:16.280 --> 00:31:19.480
and demonstrate how to use
managed services on the Google

00:31:19.480 --> 00:31:22.380
Cloud platform to perform
streaming processing

00:31:22.380 --> 00:31:24.370
and analysis.

00:31:24.370 --> 00:31:26.300
After watching this
video, you should

00:31:26.300 --> 00:31:28.040
have a clear
understanding on how

00:31:28.040 --> 00:31:30.650
to implement streaming
data processing workflows

00:31:30.650 --> 00:31:33.990
with managed services on
the Google Cloud platform.

00:31:33.990 --> 00:31:36.490
This past spring, I
was fortunate to work

00:31:36.490 --> 00:31:39.490
with Spotify's engineering
team on their streaming data

00:31:39.490 --> 00:31:42.480
processing architecture
while they were migrating

00:31:42.480 --> 00:31:44.880
to the Google Cloud platform.

00:31:44.880 --> 00:31:49.460
Now, in my spare time,
I'm also a DJ at KXP 90.3

00:31:49.460 --> 00:31:50.710
here in Seattle.

00:31:50.710 --> 00:31:54.150
And as such, I'm deeply
passionate about music.

00:31:54.150 --> 00:31:58.610
The sample for this work was
inspired by my work at KXP

00:31:58.610 --> 00:32:00.340
and my work with Spotify.

00:32:00.340 --> 00:32:03.030
I encourage you to go read
Spotify's three-part blog

00:32:03.030 --> 00:32:06.280
post on their overall data
processing architecture.

00:32:06.280 --> 00:32:10.100
You can find the series on the
Spotify Labs blog, or simply

00:32:10.100 --> 00:32:13.600
Google Spotify Event
Delivery Architecture.

00:32:13.600 --> 00:32:16.070
My sample scenario
is called Testify,

00:32:16.070 --> 00:32:19.000
and is based on very
simplified architecture.

00:32:19.000 --> 00:32:21.900
And the resulting analysis
is quite narrow, mainly

00:32:21.900 --> 00:32:24.610
not to overshadow the main
theme of this video, which

00:32:24.610 --> 00:32:27.370
is about understanding
streaming processing.

00:32:27.370 --> 00:32:30.420
The sample is based on
fabricated event log data

00:32:30.420 --> 00:32:33.370
from a mobile music
application, to which I apply

00:32:33.370 --> 00:32:35.450
several fabricated questions.

00:32:35.450 --> 00:32:36.920
And these questions
were inspired

00:32:36.920 --> 00:32:38.760
by some innovative
work that Spotify

00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:40.840
is doing around BigQuery.

00:32:40.840 --> 00:32:44.710
Now, to be clear, this sample
is about processing event data,

00:32:44.710 --> 00:32:46.910
not about streaming music.

00:32:46.910 --> 00:32:49.380
The logical model is as follows.

00:32:49.380 --> 00:32:51.440
I have a fake mobile device.

00:32:51.440 --> 00:32:53.650
And it emits events
of different meaning

00:32:53.650 --> 00:32:55.180
and of different schema.

00:32:55.180 --> 00:32:57.480
These events are
pushed to edge servers

00:32:57.480 --> 00:32:59.270
hosting applications services.

00:32:59.270 --> 00:33:01.960
And these services
emit local event logs.

00:33:01.960 --> 00:33:04.220
These logs are then
tailed in real time

00:33:04.220 --> 00:33:06.190
and ingested into a queue.

00:33:06.190 --> 00:33:09.550
And from there, I will
apply some ETL processing,

00:33:09.550 --> 00:33:13.450
and run some transforms to move
that data into a durable store

00:33:13.450 --> 00:33:16.120
for further interactive
analysis, dashboarding,

00:33:16.120 --> 00:33:18.200
and batch reporting.

00:33:18.200 --> 00:33:19.910
The architectural
concepts in this video

00:33:19.910 --> 00:33:21.660
can be applied to
nearly any business

00:33:21.660 --> 00:33:24.900
challenge across any vertical,
from fraud detection,

00:33:24.900 --> 00:33:27.420
to advertising
campaign analysis,

00:33:27.420 --> 00:33:32.500
to inventory stock-out alerting,
to real-time leaderboards.

00:33:32.500 --> 00:33:34.070
And the same
architectural concepts

00:33:34.070 --> 00:33:36.830
apply regardless of
vertical workload.

00:33:36.830 --> 00:33:38.970
I picked the music
event processing sample,

00:33:38.970 --> 00:33:39.950
because it's fun.

00:33:39.950 --> 00:33:42.910
And, well at least for me, it's
something that I really love.

00:33:42.910 --> 00:33:46.180
Now, I'd like to review a
collection of terms and tenets

00:33:46.180 --> 00:33:48.030
related to data processing.

00:33:48.030 --> 00:33:50.310
We will revisit
the testify sample.

00:33:50.310 --> 00:33:52.180
But in the meantime,
please keep some

00:33:52.180 --> 00:33:56.070
of the top level flow in mind.

00:33:56.070 --> 00:33:59.240
Now, throughout this
video, I will deliberately

00:33:59.240 --> 00:34:02.930
coalesce the concepts and
physical manifestations

00:34:02.930 --> 00:34:06.140
of data processing
and data analysis

00:34:06.140 --> 00:34:09.830
into the phrase data processing
to simplify the messaging.

00:34:09.830 --> 00:34:11.960
However, they are different.

00:34:11.960 --> 00:34:14.659
Data processing is
primarily about movement

00:34:14.659 --> 00:34:18.980
and transformation,
AKA ETL-- for example,

00:34:18.980 --> 00:34:21.730
extracting log files, and
transforming them, filtering

00:34:21.730 --> 00:34:24.690
them, and then loading
them to another location

00:34:24.690 --> 00:34:26.400
or durable store.

00:34:26.400 --> 00:34:28.800
Data analysis is
primarily about applying

00:34:28.800 --> 00:34:32.497
computation or aggregations,
things like count, sum, et

00:34:32.497 --> 00:34:36.300
cetera over group collections--
for example, performing

00:34:36.300 --> 00:34:39.270
statistical analysis
over all data elements,

00:34:39.270 --> 00:34:42.060
or sampled analysis--
for example,

00:34:42.060 --> 00:34:44.199
counting the number
of users that

00:34:44.199 --> 00:34:47.770
listen to a particular
song by hour or by minute.

00:34:47.770 --> 00:34:49.739
Now, in the context
of streaming analysis,

00:34:49.739 --> 00:34:53.350
it's also common to join
streams with other streams,

00:34:53.350 --> 00:34:57.060
and to join streams with
at rest or batch data.

00:34:57.060 --> 00:34:59.240
I make this
distinction upfront, as

00:34:59.240 --> 00:35:03.520
the architectural implications
for processing and analysis

00:35:03.520 --> 00:35:04.550
are quite different.

00:35:04.550 --> 00:35:07.540
However, in practice
they are commonly blended

00:35:07.540 --> 00:35:10.720
into the same logical
model, and in some cases

00:35:10.720 --> 00:35:13.420
the same physical system.

00:35:13.420 --> 00:35:15.220
Now, no matter what
business vertical

00:35:15.220 --> 00:35:17.940
you operate in-- finance,
health care, manufacturing,

00:35:17.940 --> 00:35:21.460
casual gaming-- or the
discipline in which you work--

00:35:21.460 --> 00:35:25.020
accounting, marketing, sales,
customer service-- the number

00:35:25.020 --> 00:35:27.190
one blocker for
growth is a concept

00:35:27.190 --> 00:35:29.950
I like to call time to answer.

00:35:29.950 --> 00:35:32.420
Specifically, how fast
can you get the answer

00:35:32.420 --> 00:35:36.250
to a question pertinent to
your business at that time?

00:35:36.250 --> 00:35:38.470
In almost all cases,
you're ultimately

00:35:38.470 --> 00:35:42.370
dependent upon data processing
and analysis systems

00:35:42.370 --> 00:35:45.760
to answer these questions.

00:35:45.760 --> 00:35:49.220
Let's say you're an advertising
analyst for a retail company.

00:35:49.220 --> 00:35:51.290
The time to answer
a key question like,

00:35:51.290 --> 00:35:55.030
what is the conversion rate on
my current marketing campaign,

00:35:55.030 --> 00:35:59.070
could mean the difference
between driving ad inventory

00:35:59.070 --> 00:36:02.880
to the right customers, or
possibly wasting dollars

00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:06.060
on already stocked out products.

00:36:06.060 --> 00:36:09.350
If you're in the manufacturing
space focused on production

00:36:09.350 --> 00:36:12.620
line efficiency, knowing the
answer to a question like,

00:36:12.620 --> 00:36:15.520
what is the current distribution
of to-be-processed processed

00:36:15.520 --> 00:36:18.850
orders, and how do those orders
align to my existing machine

00:36:18.850 --> 00:36:20.320
setup?

00:36:20.320 --> 00:36:23.160
This could be the difference
between positive and negative

00:36:23.160 --> 00:36:25.890
margins on your
next production run.

00:36:25.890 --> 00:36:29.770
Or a question like, if you're
a casual game developer,

00:36:29.770 --> 00:36:31.750
what percentage
of users recently

00:36:31.750 --> 00:36:35.880
abandoned my game as a result
of introducing new game logic?

00:36:35.880 --> 00:36:38.470
The time to answer here
could have massive impact

00:36:38.470 --> 00:36:40.230
on your daily active users.

00:36:40.230 --> 00:36:43.100
Or maybe you become the
focus of the next episode

00:36:43.100 --> 00:36:44.970
of Silicon Valley.

00:36:44.970 --> 00:36:48.050
Now, the theme across
these questions is speed.

00:36:48.050 --> 00:36:50.660
I've never met a business
or technical decision

00:36:50.660 --> 00:36:54.020
maker that said, I'd really like
to have questions to my answers

00:36:54.020 --> 00:36:55.660
with more delay.

00:36:55.660 --> 00:36:57.630
The converse is true.

00:36:57.630 --> 00:37:00.640
It's the true state of business
today, now more than ever.

00:37:00.640 --> 00:37:02.850
Those who can answer
questions faster

00:37:02.850 --> 00:37:05.510
are more likely to
succeed and grow.

00:37:05.510 --> 00:37:09.920
The challenge is that growth
creates more data, and in turn

00:37:09.920 --> 00:37:13.290
creates more questions, thus
more pressure on processing

00:37:13.290 --> 00:37:15.020
and analysis systems.

00:37:15.020 --> 00:37:17.350
And when implemented
incorrectly,

00:37:17.350 --> 00:37:20.750
these underlying systems
increase the time to answer.

00:37:20.750 --> 00:37:22.790
Time to answer matters.

00:37:22.790 --> 00:37:25.750
Now, for decades businesses have
been using various techniques

00:37:25.750 --> 00:37:29.840
for data processing, batch being
one of the most common patterns

00:37:29.840 --> 00:37:33.110
to answer questions like the
ones I've previously raised.

00:37:33.110 --> 00:37:37.300
While effective, batch
processing on fixed capacity,

00:37:37.300 --> 00:37:42.370
non-managed environments present
risks related to cost control,

00:37:42.370 --> 00:37:46.480
increased time to answer,
and answer accuracy,

00:37:46.480 --> 00:37:49.510
or rather answer inaccuracy.

00:37:49.510 --> 00:37:51.660
Let's look at a simple example.

00:37:51.660 --> 00:37:54.050
Here, we have a
fixed data set, which

00:37:54.050 --> 00:37:56.950
will be processed via
batch job on a fixed

00:37:56.950 --> 00:37:59.340
cluster of resources.

00:37:59.340 --> 00:38:03.070
When these resources are ready,
and the job schedule time is

00:38:03.070 --> 00:38:05.350
hit to start, the job starts.

00:38:05.350 --> 00:38:07.160
The job and the
resources are effectively

00:38:07.160 --> 00:38:09.950
blocked until the
job is completed.

00:38:09.950 --> 00:38:12.970
After the job is completed,
results are admitted.

00:38:12.970 --> 00:38:15.290
There is an effective
clock time for this job.

00:38:15.290 --> 00:38:17.210
This is, in essence,
the time to answer.

00:38:17.210 --> 00:38:20.780
For example, this job took
30 clock minutes to run,

00:38:20.780 --> 00:38:24.870
and consumed a total of 200
minutes of resource time.

00:38:24.870 --> 00:38:26.600
Now, we're going to
extend the example.

00:38:26.600 --> 00:38:28.390
Let's add a new job.

00:38:28.390 --> 00:38:30.980
In this case, the new
job will be blocked when

00:38:30.980 --> 00:38:33.050
the existing job is running.

00:38:33.050 --> 00:38:37.470
The typical response here is
to add more resources, CPU,

00:38:37.470 --> 00:38:40.190
memory, disk, and
network capacity,

00:38:40.190 --> 00:38:43.880
and deploy the job to these
new specific resources.

00:38:43.880 --> 00:38:46.460
Now, one of the challenges
is that you end up

00:38:46.460 --> 00:38:48.250
potentially wasting resources.

00:38:48.250 --> 00:38:50.580
Notice that the previous
job is now complete.

00:38:50.580 --> 00:38:53.490
And the initial
resources are now idle.

00:38:53.490 --> 00:38:55.110
Now that the second
job is complete,

00:38:55.110 --> 00:38:58.810
our entire collection
of resources is idle.

00:38:58.810 --> 00:39:00.840
If you have experience
with Hadoop clusters,

00:39:00.840 --> 00:39:02.380
you might be
thinking, well, this

00:39:02.380 --> 00:39:05.420
is what the resource
manager and YARN are for?

00:39:05.420 --> 00:39:08.450
And, yes, they can be highly
effective at optimizing

00:39:08.450 --> 00:39:11.590
resources based on
scheduling semantics.

00:39:11.590 --> 00:39:14.600
However, the issue
here is that if there

00:39:14.600 --> 00:39:17.880
are no jobs to be scheduled,
the resources will remain idle.

00:39:17.880 --> 00:39:22.180
And in some cases, they just
may be better to be torn down.

00:39:22.180 --> 00:39:23.990
The other challenge
is that if you

00:39:23.990 --> 00:39:26.570
need to increased the
capacity on the cluster,

00:39:26.570 --> 00:39:28.240
in an unmanaged
environment you're

00:39:28.240 --> 00:39:31.060
going to have to physically
deploy and manage the lifecycle

00:39:31.060 --> 00:39:33.400
of those resources.

00:39:33.400 --> 00:39:36.790
Let's take a look at the
first job scenario again.

00:39:36.790 --> 00:39:38.850
Now, at the time that
the job was scheduled,

00:39:38.850 --> 00:39:41.170
the input data is
effectively locked.

00:39:41.170 --> 00:39:42.720
In relational
database terms, you

00:39:42.720 --> 00:39:44.580
could think about this
as an effective page

00:39:44.580 --> 00:39:47.340
lock over the entire data.

00:39:47.340 --> 00:39:50.310
However, in this case,
some data arrives late

00:39:50.310 --> 00:39:52.970
after the job is started.

00:39:52.970 --> 00:39:56.810
And it was really supposed to be
part of the original input set.

00:39:56.810 --> 00:39:59.750
Now, the job finishes,
output is created.

00:39:59.750 --> 00:40:02.050
But the output is now
only partially correct,

00:40:02.050 --> 00:40:04.130
or possibly
completely inaccurate,

00:40:04.130 --> 00:40:07.020
depending on the impact
of the late data.

00:40:07.020 --> 00:40:10.150
There are many techniques
to deal with this problem.

00:40:10.150 --> 00:40:12.620
Now, if the job computation
here is associative,

00:40:12.620 --> 00:40:15.360
one could rerun the job
against the new data,

00:40:15.360 --> 00:40:17.840
and add the results to
the original job output.

00:40:17.840 --> 00:40:20.270
Or one could delete
the original results

00:40:20.270 --> 00:40:22.530
and rerun the entire job again.

00:40:22.530 --> 00:40:26.480
Each approach has its
own challenges and risks.

00:40:26.480 --> 00:40:30.290
To be clear, I'm not saying that
all batch processing is bad,

00:40:30.290 --> 00:40:32.890
or that all unmanaged
data processing is bad.

00:40:32.890 --> 00:40:35.720
I simply want to point out that
there are inherent challenges

00:40:35.720 --> 00:40:37.100
for each approach.

00:40:37.100 --> 00:40:39.720
And depending on your
use cases, data volumes,

00:40:39.720 --> 00:40:42.890
analysis requirements,
and resource budget,

00:40:42.890 --> 00:40:45.820
you may encounter these issues.

00:40:45.820 --> 00:40:48.360
The next several concepts
I'm going to talk about

00:40:48.360 --> 00:40:51.160
are grafted from two blog
posts, recently written

00:40:51.160 --> 00:40:53.780
by an amazing software
engineer, and also

00:40:53.780 --> 00:40:57.440
the technical lead for the
MillWheel team here at Google.

00:40:57.440 --> 00:40:59.910
His name is Tyler Akidau.

00:40:59.910 --> 00:41:01.710
I encourage you to go read them.

00:41:01.710 --> 00:41:04.460
Simply Google, streaming
101, and they show up

00:41:04.460 --> 00:41:07.230
at the top of the stack
because they're highly relevant

00:41:07.230 --> 00:41:08.830
and they're highly popular.

00:41:08.830 --> 00:41:10.980
In the previous
unmanaged batch section,

00:41:10.980 --> 00:41:13.930
I talked about
late arriving data,

00:41:13.930 --> 00:41:17.130
and its impact on the
output of the batch job.

00:41:17.130 --> 00:41:19.660
As I start to talk about
streaming processing,

00:41:19.660 --> 00:41:23.430
I want to establish a
distinction between the data--

00:41:23.430 --> 00:41:27.660
specifically its boundedness--
and the processing approach.

00:41:27.660 --> 00:41:30.610
This concept is super
helpful in understanding

00:41:30.610 --> 00:41:33.610
the valuable behaviors
of a streaming system,

00:41:33.610 --> 00:41:36.270
and how they might be able to
be applied to your processing

00:41:36.270 --> 00:41:38.210
problems.

00:41:38.210 --> 00:41:40.660
So let's talk about boundedness.

00:41:40.660 --> 00:41:42.700
Bounded data is finite.

00:41:42.700 --> 00:41:44.040
It's at rest.

00:41:44.040 --> 00:41:45.620
It does not change.

00:41:45.620 --> 00:41:49.620
For example, you may have 10
terabytes of historical web

00:41:49.620 --> 00:41:52.945
log data, specifically
in files, in Avro format,

00:41:52.945 --> 00:41:57.840
or JSON, sitting at
rest in a blob store.

00:41:57.840 --> 00:42:00.360
Unbounded data is infinite.

00:42:00.360 --> 00:42:02.930
It's constantly changing.

00:42:02.930 --> 00:42:05.700
It's driven by frequency.

00:42:05.700 --> 00:42:09.740
It's driven by changes in size,
and potentially in schema.

00:42:09.740 --> 00:42:12.340
And the time associated
with the data in the stream

00:42:12.340 --> 00:42:14.340
may be relevant
to the event time,

00:42:14.340 --> 00:42:16.170
or to its processing time.

00:42:16.170 --> 00:42:19.410
For example, you may be
listening to a TCP socket,

00:42:19.410 --> 00:42:21.550
and reading all the
web log data coming

00:42:21.550 --> 00:42:23.310
from all of your servers.

00:42:23.310 --> 00:42:27.570
Processing, specifically in
this case bounded processing,

00:42:27.570 --> 00:42:30.980
think of this as classic
batch processing, specifically

00:42:30.980 --> 00:42:35.070
a processing engine
applied to bounded data.

00:42:35.070 --> 00:42:36.750
And the same for
unbounded-- this

00:42:36.750 --> 00:42:41.400
is a specific mode of processing
applied to an unbound source.

00:42:41.400 --> 00:42:43.680
It's important to understand
these definitions,

00:42:43.680 --> 00:42:45.510
as their underlying
implementations

00:42:45.510 --> 00:42:49.410
create specific
architectural boundaries.

00:42:49.410 --> 00:42:52.920
In my previous batch example,
the data was, in fact,

00:42:52.920 --> 00:42:54.030
unbounded.

00:42:54.030 --> 00:42:57.670
However, the system was
treating it as bounded data.

00:42:57.670 --> 00:42:59.800
This is a very
common pattern, where

00:42:59.800 --> 00:43:04.350
one would run periodic batch
jobs over unbounded data-- say,

00:43:04.350 --> 00:43:07.500
contained in a relational
database like SQL Server,

00:43:07.500 --> 00:43:10.870
or a NoSQL store like Mongo.

00:43:10.870 --> 00:43:12.830
It's a very valid approach.

00:43:12.830 --> 00:43:14.880
But it can lead to
correctness issues,

00:43:14.880 --> 00:43:19.810
or make a pattern like session
analysis very challenging.

00:43:19.810 --> 00:43:22.270
In addition to boundedness,
the time domain

00:43:22.270 --> 00:43:25.190
associated with data
can have a major impact

00:43:25.190 --> 00:43:28.520
on how data is processed and the
correctness of the processing

00:43:28.520 --> 00:43:29.290
output.

00:43:29.290 --> 00:43:33.190
Event time is an explicit time
associated with a data element

00:43:33.190 --> 00:43:36.310
or event tied to the
clock time of when

00:43:36.310 --> 00:43:38.620
and where the event occurred.

00:43:38.620 --> 00:43:41.210
In most cases, the time
is highly distributed,

00:43:41.210 --> 00:43:42.930
and potentially volatile.

00:43:42.930 --> 00:43:46.030
Processing time is the time
associated with a processing

00:43:46.030 --> 00:43:47.130
engine.

00:43:47.130 --> 00:43:49.490
In some cases, it
might be the time

00:43:49.490 --> 00:43:51.650
the engine ingested the data.

00:43:51.650 --> 00:43:54.440
In others, it might
be the time associated

00:43:54.440 --> 00:43:58.380
with the step or stage being
executed within the engine.

00:43:58.380 --> 00:44:02.000
In most cases, this time is
centralized, or synchronized,

00:44:02.000 --> 00:44:03.740
with a common time service.

00:44:03.740 --> 00:44:05.500
Let's use an example.

00:44:05.500 --> 00:44:10.200
I just played Justin Bieber
on my testify application

00:44:10.200 --> 00:44:15.120
at 12:14 GMT on July 25th, 2016.

00:44:15.120 --> 00:44:17.430
That was the event time.

00:44:17.430 --> 00:44:20.850
However, this event was
processed three minutes later

00:44:20.850 --> 00:44:23.690
by my streaming
processing engine.

00:44:23.690 --> 00:44:26.150
Now, the distinction between
event time and processing time

00:44:26.150 --> 00:44:28.500
here is important,
because it will ultimately

00:44:28.500 --> 00:44:31.770
impact the correctness
of my processing output.

00:44:31.770 --> 00:44:34.950
For example, if I was trying
to count plays by minute,

00:44:34.950 --> 00:44:37.530
my results would be
skewed by three minutes

00:44:37.530 --> 00:44:40.436
if I was using processing time.

00:44:40.436 --> 00:44:41.810
Now, what I really
wanted to know

00:44:41.810 --> 00:44:44.160
is I want to do count
of plays by minute,

00:44:44.160 --> 00:44:46.980
were time is relative
to the actual play time,

00:44:46.980 --> 00:44:48.820
not processing time.

00:44:48.820 --> 00:44:51.910
Now, the minimum delta between
event time and processing time

00:44:51.910 --> 00:44:54.100
is relative to the
speed of light,

00:44:54.100 --> 00:44:59.940
plus some additional latency,
which we'll talk about next.

00:44:59.940 --> 00:45:02.920
Regardless of batch
or streaming modes,

00:45:02.920 --> 00:45:04.790
if you're dealing
with unbounded data

00:45:04.790 --> 00:45:07.640
and you want to process
data based on event time,

00:45:07.640 --> 00:45:10.860
you need to understand
your latency exposure,

00:45:10.860 --> 00:45:14.990
and then employ techniques to
deal with late arriving data.

00:45:14.990 --> 00:45:18.000
In a perfect world,
latency would be zero.

00:45:18.000 --> 00:45:21.880
Well, it would at least be
bounded by the speed of light.

00:45:21.880 --> 00:45:24.530
Or it would be nice if
you could understand

00:45:24.530 --> 00:45:28.210
and have some type of fixed
overall system latency.

00:45:28.210 --> 00:45:31.110
But we don't live
in a perfect world.

00:45:31.110 --> 00:45:34.220
In my testify example,
there are three areas

00:45:34.220 --> 00:45:36.190
that could create latency.

00:45:36.190 --> 00:45:39.450
On the transmission side
from my phone to the cloud,

00:45:39.450 --> 00:45:43.140
it could be something as simple
as network unavailability.

00:45:43.140 --> 00:45:46.520
Or it could be something
very downstream,

00:45:46.520 --> 00:45:50.020
like a slow disk that
is backing a cue.

00:45:50.020 --> 00:45:55.430
And those reads are now reduced
on the throughput side, which

00:45:55.430 --> 00:45:56.880
would create latency.

00:45:56.880 --> 00:45:59.060
In almost all cases,
you don't have control

00:45:59.060 --> 00:46:00.700
over these potential issues.

00:46:00.700 --> 00:46:04.420
But the best thing you can do is
plan for what level of latency

00:46:04.420 --> 00:46:06.240
you could expect,
and that you're

00:46:06.240 --> 00:46:08.810
willing to tolerate, and
have a plan with how you

00:46:08.810 --> 00:46:11.240
deal with late arriving data.

00:46:11.240 --> 00:46:14.480
I'll talk more about how you
deal with late arriving data

00:46:14.480 --> 00:46:16.240
in the next section.

00:46:16.240 --> 00:46:20.370
Now that we understand
events versus processing time

00:46:20.370 --> 00:46:23.820
and the impact of latency, we
need an additional primitive

00:46:23.820 --> 00:46:27.730
to rationalize time
into manageable groups.

00:46:27.730 --> 00:46:30.830
Having the ability to window
data into finite chunks

00:46:30.830 --> 00:46:34.790
enables computation that
would otherwise be impossible.

00:46:34.790 --> 00:46:37.640
For example,
without windowing it

00:46:37.640 --> 00:46:39.490
would be very
difficult to perform

00:46:39.490 --> 00:46:43.740
even a simple aggregation,
like count or sum,

00:46:43.740 --> 00:46:45.800
over unbounded data.

00:46:45.800 --> 00:46:48.240
In essence, you need
a mechanism to group

00:46:48.240 --> 00:46:50.430
all events into
a window in order

00:46:50.430 --> 00:46:53.040
to apply an aggregation
to a window that

00:46:53.040 --> 00:46:56.150
has some level of
data completeness.

00:46:56.150 --> 00:46:58.620
In classic MapReduce,
you'd create

00:46:58.620 --> 00:47:02.190
keys that were tied to
start and ends of Windows.

00:47:02.190 --> 00:47:04.510
In other system, say
like Apache Storm,

00:47:04.510 --> 00:47:08.020
you could set the
time stamp for tuple.

00:47:08.020 --> 00:47:11.560
And then that time stamp is
propagated into a window.

00:47:11.560 --> 00:47:15.210
Now, not all processing systems
support all window types.

00:47:15.210 --> 00:47:18.400
I'll talk a little bit later
about specific windowing

00:47:18.400 --> 00:47:21.780
support in different
processing engines.

00:47:21.780 --> 00:47:24.450
Before I stop
talking about time,

00:47:24.450 --> 00:47:29.220
I need to cover another
important concept that

00:47:29.220 --> 00:47:31.500
is tightly bound
to latency, which

00:47:31.500 --> 00:47:34.190
is the concept of a watermark.

00:47:34.190 --> 00:47:37.210
Here, you can see event
time on the x-axis,

00:47:37.210 --> 00:47:40.200
and processing
time on the y-axis.

00:47:40.200 --> 00:47:42.990
In a perfect, zero
latency world,

00:47:42.990 --> 00:47:46.240
we could use an ideal
watermark that moves linearly

00:47:46.240 --> 00:47:47.470
tied to clock time.

00:47:47.470 --> 00:47:49.200
But the world is not fair.

00:47:49.200 --> 00:47:50.950
And latency is everywhere.

00:47:50.950 --> 00:47:56.050
Thus, if we're going to perform
processing based on event time,

00:47:56.050 --> 00:47:58.780
we need a heuristic-driven
watermark.

00:47:58.780 --> 00:48:01.480
Now, this watermark
is an estimate

00:48:01.480 --> 00:48:04.380
based on various
techniques-- for example,

00:48:04.380 --> 00:48:08.260
a simple distribution sampler,
or something more sophisticated

00:48:08.260 --> 00:48:11.370
like looking at the
oldest unprocessed message

00:48:11.370 --> 00:48:12.950
in an upstream queue.

00:48:12.950 --> 00:48:16.650
Now, if the heuristic is too
slow, results are delayed.

00:48:16.650 --> 00:48:21.180
If the heuristic is too fast,
results may be marked late.

00:48:21.180 --> 00:48:25.710
Now, in this example, I'm doing
some fixed window analysis.

00:48:25.710 --> 00:48:29.570
Notice that in the event time
window of 12:01 to 12:02,

00:48:29.570 --> 00:48:32.020
there is an event of value 9.

00:48:32.020 --> 00:48:36.300
But it's been marked as red,
because it was processed way

00:48:36.300 --> 00:48:38.370
past the watermark.

00:48:38.370 --> 00:48:39.580
It was marked late.

00:48:39.580 --> 00:48:42.102
The question is, what do
you do with late data?

00:48:42.102 --> 00:48:43.560
I'm going to talk
a little bit more

00:48:43.560 --> 00:48:45.690
later about how to
deal with late data,

00:48:45.690 --> 00:48:48.020
as well as how to trigger
speculative results

00:48:48.020 --> 00:48:49.640
within a window.

00:48:49.640 --> 00:48:53.010
We can now tie all
these concepts together,

00:48:53.010 --> 00:48:56.190
and end on a crisp
definition of streaming.

00:48:56.190 --> 00:48:59.700
Streaming is an execution
engine, system, service,

00:48:59.700 --> 00:49:03.330
runner capable of
processing unbounded data.

00:49:03.330 --> 00:49:06.660
And when designed correctly,
a streaming processing engine

00:49:06.660 --> 00:49:10.790
can provide low latency,
speculative or partial results,

00:49:10.790 --> 00:49:13.920
the ability to flexibly
reason about time,

00:49:13.920 --> 00:49:15.960
provide controls
for correctness,

00:49:15.960 --> 00:49:20.540
and ultimately the power to
perform complex analysis.

00:49:20.540 --> 00:49:23.460
Now that I have the basic
terms and tenets laid out

00:49:23.460 --> 00:49:25.300
for streaming
processing, I'm going

00:49:25.300 --> 00:49:28.510
to use several managed services
on the Google Cloud platform

00:49:28.510 --> 00:49:31.140
to build a streaming data
processing application.

00:49:31.140 --> 00:49:35.050
Specifically, I'm going to use
Cloud Pub/Sub for ingestion,

00:49:35.050 --> 00:49:38.670
Cloud Dataflow for processing
and imperative analysis,

00:49:38.670 --> 00:49:43.410
and BigQuery for durable storage
and interactive analysis.

00:49:43.410 --> 00:49:47.000
Cloud Pub/Sub is a managed
service for streaming data

00:49:47.000 --> 00:49:49.586
on the Google Cloud platform.

00:49:49.586 --> 00:49:52.720
Cloud Pub/Sub enables durable,
asynchronous messaging

00:49:52.720 --> 00:49:57.480
applications, including patterns
for one to one, one to many,

00:49:57.480 --> 00:49:59.190
and many to many.

00:49:59.190 --> 00:50:02.770
Cloud Pub/Sub is globally
available with large message

00:50:02.770 --> 00:50:05.970
size support, up to
10 megabytes per put.

00:50:05.970 --> 00:50:07.670
And Cloud Pub/Sub
club provides at least

00:50:07.670 --> 00:50:11.070
once delivery with low
latency and on demand

00:50:11.070 --> 00:50:14.870
scalability to one million
messages per second,

00:50:14.870 --> 00:50:16.250
and beyond.

00:50:16.250 --> 00:50:19.330
Now, Pub/Sub is technically
a streaming engine,

00:50:19.330 --> 00:50:21.600
as it can process
unbounded data.

00:50:21.600 --> 00:50:25.060
However, it does not provide
mechanisms for reasoning

00:50:25.060 --> 00:50:28.330
about time, or the ability
to express computation

00:50:28.330 --> 00:50:29.930
over the stream.

00:50:29.930 --> 00:50:32.070
We will use other
services to perform

00:50:32.070 --> 00:50:34.110
processing and analysis.

00:50:34.110 --> 00:50:38.220
As you can see, Pub/Sub is a
hub to enable streams to flow

00:50:38.220 --> 00:50:40.330
in and out of GCP services.

00:50:40.330 --> 00:50:44.970
Moreover, it can be connected
to any service on the cloud,

00:50:44.970 --> 00:50:50.970
or on premise via HTTP, or
directly over sockets via GRPC.

00:50:50.970 --> 00:50:53.890
Pub/Sub uses a simple
topic subscription

00:50:53.890 --> 00:50:57.670
model to enable fan in and
fan out messaging topologies.

00:50:57.670 --> 00:51:01.000
In my testify example,
I may route all events

00:51:01.000 --> 00:51:02.360
to a single topic.

00:51:02.360 --> 00:51:04.920
Or I could split event
types across topics

00:51:04.920 --> 00:51:08.350
and then multiplex them
to multiple subscribers.

00:51:08.350 --> 00:51:12.800
Here is a zoomed view of my
testified demo application.

00:51:12.800 --> 00:51:15.150
I'm going to show you how
to setup a Cloud Pub/Sub

00:51:15.150 --> 00:51:18.700
topic to ingest an unbounded
stream, which will then

00:51:18.700 --> 00:51:21.830
be processed by Cloud Dataflow.

00:51:21.830 --> 00:51:24.240
In this case, I'll
be using one topic

00:51:24.240 --> 00:51:26.070
to ingest a stream
of events being

00:51:26.070 --> 00:51:31.160
generated by a data fabricator,
which mimics log creation.

00:51:31.160 --> 00:51:34.040
There are several ways to
interact with Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:51:34.040 --> 00:51:37.330
You can use the REST API, one
of the Google Cloud client

00:51:37.330 --> 00:51:41.390
language libraries, or for
basic management operations

00:51:41.390 --> 00:51:43.890
you can use the
Google Cloud console.

00:51:43.890 --> 00:51:46.960
You can see that I have two
existing topics in my Google

00:51:46.960 --> 00:51:48.380
Cloud console.

00:51:48.380 --> 00:51:50.620
I'll create a new topic
called Testify Demo.

00:51:56.840 --> 00:51:58.780
Now, once I hit
Create, this topic

00:51:58.780 --> 00:52:02.880
is globally available to receive
messages at a default rate

00:52:02.880 --> 00:52:06.500
up to 100 megabytes per
second, with a maximum message

00:52:06.500 --> 00:52:08.650
size of 10 megabytes.

00:52:08.650 --> 00:52:11.800
If you need additional quota,
please follow the instructions

00:52:11.800 --> 00:52:15.430
in the quota section of the
Cloud Pub/Sub documentation.

00:52:15.430 --> 00:52:18.230
Now, you'll notice that there is
no forced sharding or bucketing

00:52:18.230 --> 00:52:19.640
when you setup your topic.

00:52:19.640 --> 00:52:22.290
Simply give it a
name, call create,

00:52:22.290 --> 00:52:24.960
and then start publishing data.

00:52:24.960 --> 00:52:27.760
Now let's look at something a
little bit more interesting.

00:52:27.760 --> 00:52:31.340
I previously created a
topic called Testify Events.

00:52:31.340 --> 00:52:34.410
This is receiving my
fabricated event data.

00:52:34.410 --> 00:52:37.330
This topic has two
active subscriptions.

00:52:37.330 --> 00:52:39.900
These were created by
my Cloud Dataflow job

00:52:39.900 --> 00:52:42.180
reading the event stream.

00:52:42.180 --> 00:52:45.280
Now, let's look at this topic
in my Management Console

00:52:45.280 --> 00:52:47.740
called Stackdriver.

00:52:47.740 --> 00:52:50.040
Stackdriver is a real-time
monitoring, logging,

00:52:50.040 --> 00:52:54.000
and diagnostic service
for Google Cloud, AWS,

00:52:54.000 --> 00:52:56.180
and open source packages.

00:52:56.180 --> 00:53:00.490
Here, I created a dashboard
for my Pub/Sub resources

00:53:00.490 --> 00:53:05.880
showing four graphs-- my
publish rate, my pull rate,

00:53:05.880 --> 00:53:08.470
the number of
undelivered messages,

00:53:08.470 --> 00:53:11.920
and the oldest
unacknowledged message time.

00:53:11.920 --> 00:53:13.770
You can create your
own graphs based

00:53:13.770 --> 00:53:16.240
on event rate,
event size, errors,

00:53:16.240 --> 00:53:17.850
and many other dimensions.

00:53:17.850 --> 00:53:21.830
I built these graphs in order
to monitor the overall lag

00:53:21.830 --> 00:53:24.540
in my event processing system.

00:53:24.540 --> 00:53:29.460
Now, I'm publishing right now
at approximately 101 to 100,000

00:53:29.460 --> 00:53:31.910
events per second.

00:53:31.910 --> 00:53:34.100
And I'm reading,
or pulling them,

00:53:34.100 --> 00:53:36.300
at roughly the same rates.

00:53:36.300 --> 00:53:38.840
And my undelivered
message count is

00:53:38.840 --> 00:53:44.070
right around between 150
and 200,000 messages.

00:53:44.070 --> 00:53:48.040
Cloud Pub/Sub has a built-in
seven day retention policy

00:53:48.040 --> 00:53:50.010
for undelivered messages.

00:53:50.010 --> 00:53:53.160
If I were to, say, shut
down my downstream reader--

00:53:53.160 --> 00:53:56.040
in this case, Cloud
Dataflow-- all the messages

00:53:56.040 --> 00:53:59.010
would be durably
stored for seven days.

00:53:59.010 --> 00:54:03.910
Now, one last highlight that
is helpful for Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:54:03.910 --> 00:54:08.230
as many other workflows like
complete, storage, et cetera,

00:54:08.230 --> 00:54:11.590
is the ability to create
custom alerting policies which

00:54:11.590 --> 00:54:14.670
can monitor specific
metrics and thresholds,

00:54:14.670 --> 00:54:17.620
and then send you notifications.

00:54:17.620 --> 00:54:24.440
Here, I had created a policy
to check a particular metric.

00:54:24.440 --> 00:54:28.270
In this case, I wanted to
look at messages published

00:54:28.270 --> 00:54:30.910
for my Testify
Events topic, if it

00:54:30.910 --> 00:54:35.080
was above a threshold of
50,000 events per second

00:54:35.080 --> 00:54:36.670
for greater than one minute.

00:54:36.670 --> 00:54:39.380
And in this, case it
would alert the dashboard,

00:54:39.380 --> 00:54:41.680
as well as send me an email.

00:54:41.680 --> 00:54:44.300
Now that you understand the
basics of Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:54:44.300 --> 00:54:47.340
and we've setup a topic, and
showed you some management

00:54:47.340 --> 00:54:48.480
techniques.

00:54:48.480 --> 00:54:51.360
And we have a live topic
and subscription running.

00:54:51.360 --> 00:54:55.850
Let's look at how Cloud Dataflow
is processing the stream.

00:54:55.850 --> 00:54:58.880
Google Cloud Dataflow is
a managed data processing

00:54:58.880 --> 00:55:01.710
service, and an open
source programming

00:55:01.710 --> 00:55:04.120
model that unifies
batch and streaming

00:55:04.120 --> 00:55:06.720
processing into one model.

00:55:06.720 --> 00:55:08.930
The Cloud Dataflow
service provides

00:55:08.930 --> 00:55:13.100
on demand resource deployment,
and management on a per job

00:55:13.100 --> 00:55:13.940
basis.

00:55:13.940 --> 00:55:16.310
This means you can scale
your data processing

00:55:16.310 --> 00:55:21.240
without resource contention and
limits found in fixed clusters.

00:55:21.240 --> 00:55:24.310
The service automatically
scales resources up and down

00:55:24.310 --> 00:55:27.080
for both batch and
streaming jobs,

00:55:27.080 --> 00:55:31.090
freeing you from under or
over-provisioning resources,

00:55:31.090 --> 00:55:34.450
and enabling you to optimize
your time to answer.

00:55:34.450 --> 00:55:36.240
The Cloud Dataflow
programming model

00:55:36.240 --> 00:55:39.040
was the basis for
the now open source

00:55:39.040 --> 00:55:41.450
Apache Software
Foundation incubator

00:55:41.450 --> 00:55:45.090
project called Apache Beam.

00:55:45.090 --> 00:55:49.100
Functionally, Cloud Dataflow
unifies batch and streaming

00:55:49.100 --> 00:55:51.110
into one model.

00:55:51.110 --> 00:55:54.290
It enables processing in
processing time space,

00:55:54.290 --> 00:55:56.360
as well as event time space.

00:55:56.360 --> 00:55:59.660
It provides a window [INAUDIBLE]
with support for fixed sliding

00:55:59.660 --> 00:56:04.040
and session windows that support
advanced triggering mechanisms

00:56:04.040 --> 00:56:06.420
for speculative window
emission, and to deal

00:56:06.420 --> 00:56:08.440
with late arriving data.

00:56:08.440 --> 00:56:10.580
The Cloud Dataflow
managed service

00:56:10.580 --> 00:56:15.280
is geared at driving a no ops,
or no operations deployment

00:56:15.280 --> 00:56:17.420
and management model.

00:56:17.420 --> 00:56:20.670
Instead of you spending
time downloading packages,

00:56:20.670 --> 00:56:24.640
spinning up compute resources,
tuning cluster configuration,

00:56:24.640 --> 00:56:26.890
and dealing with
control plane issues,

00:56:26.890 --> 00:56:31.280
you simply code your pipeline,
and submit your pipeline

00:56:31.280 --> 00:56:32.820
as a job to the service.

00:56:32.820 --> 00:56:35.380
And it handles the rest.

00:56:35.380 --> 00:56:37.920
You're only charged for the
time that your job executes

00:56:37.920 --> 00:56:39.100
on the service.

00:56:39.100 --> 00:56:42.740
Since resources are deployed and
managed completely on demand,

00:56:42.740 --> 00:56:45.710
you can reduce the capital
cost of deploying and managing

00:56:45.710 --> 00:56:48.370
long running fixed
capacity clusters.

00:56:48.370 --> 00:56:50.370
The service can
seamlessly transition

00:56:50.370 --> 00:56:52.620
between batch and
streaming processing,

00:56:52.620 --> 00:56:54.940
providing a single
service to handle

00:56:54.940 --> 00:56:57.960
bounded and unbounded
data processing.

00:56:57.960 --> 00:57:00.820
Now, with the forthcoming
Apache Beam support,

00:57:00.820 --> 00:57:02.930
you'll be able to
migrate your data flow

00:57:02.930 --> 00:57:08.790
jobs across Apache Spark,
Apache Flink, and other runners.

00:57:08.790 --> 00:57:12.620
Here is a logical view of a
simple Cloud Dataflow pipeline

00:57:12.620 --> 00:57:14.850
from my testify workflow.

00:57:14.850 --> 00:57:17.000
You submit the
pipeline as a job.

00:57:17.000 --> 00:57:19.500
And the service will then
deploy the worker resources

00:57:19.500 --> 00:57:20.630
and schedule the work.

00:57:20.630 --> 00:57:23.260
In this case, I start
with a bounded source.

00:57:23.260 --> 00:57:26.990
I read the log data, apply
some simple transformations,

00:57:26.990 --> 00:57:28.490
and then write it
to various shards.

00:57:32.060 --> 00:57:35.030
Now, let's migrate this
to an unbounded source,

00:57:35.030 --> 00:57:38.850
and turn this into
a streaming process.

00:57:38.850 --> 00:57:40.700
Nothing changes
with the pipeline,

00:57:40.700 --> 00:57:43.480
other than changing the source.

00:57:43.480 --> 00:57:45.490
Finally, let's add
some windowing,

00:57:45.490 --> 00:57:48.470
and add a simple aggregator--
in this case a count.

00:57:48.470 --> 00:57:51.740
I could continue to add more and
more branches to the pipeline.

00:57:51.740 --> 00:57:53.440
I could split the graph.

00:57:53.440 --> 00:57:56.720
I could apply varying
windows, and even

00:57:56.720 --> 00:58:00.480
apply more sophisticated
aggregations.

00:58:00.480 --> 00:58:03.340
This is a view of my running
Cloud Dataflow streaming

00:58:03.340 --> 00:58:05.750
job, which is processing
the events published

00:58:05.750 --> 00:58:10.110
into my Pub/Sub, and flowing
to related subscriptions.

00:58:10.110 --> 00:58:15.070
It has been running for a little
over two hours and 40 minutes.

00:58:15.070 --> 00:58:21.320
And it is processed
around 971 million events.

00:58:21.320 --> 00:58:24.460
The graph starts by reading
event data from Pub/Sub.

00:58:24.460 --> 00:58:27.450
This event data is
text CSV formatted.

00:58:27.450 --> 00:58:30.240
You could also process
JSON, Avro XML,

00:58:30.240 --> 00:58:32.460
or create your
own custom format.

00:58:32.460 --> 00:58:35.960
Of important note-- I'm using
event time as my time domain,

00:58:35.960 --> 00:58:37.770
not processing time.

00:58:37.770 --> 00:58:39.540
To do this, I tell
Dataflow to look

00:58:39.540 --> 00:58:42.110
for a specific label
on each Pub/Sub message

00:58:42.110 --> 00:58:43.670
called timestamp.

00:58:43.670 --> 00:58:46.510
From there, Dataflow
handles the calculation

00:58:46.510 --> 00:58:50.840
of the data watermark, which
as the watermark advances

00:58:50.840 --> 00:58:54.970
any window ending before or
at that time will be closed.

00:58:54.970 --> 00:58:56.790
I'm using event
time here in order

00:58:56.790 --> 00:59:00.640
to provide a higher level of
accuracy for my aggregations.

00:59:00.640 --> 00:59:02.700
If I were to use
processing time,

00:59:02.700 --> 00:59:05.800
I never could be guaranteed
to have all the data relevant

00:59:05.800 --> 00:59:09.490
to a specific event time window.

00:59:09.490 --> 00:59:12.430
After reading the
raw stream data,

00:59:12.430 --> 00:59:14.540
data is sent to parse transform.

00:59:14.540 --> 00:59:19.790
This transform has two steps--
parse and de-serialize.

00:59:19.790 --> 00:59:23.590
De-serialize maps the parsed
CSV data into an object

00:59:23.590 --> 00:59:26.410
called Event.

00:59:26.410 --> 00:59:29.840
From here, the job
has three main goals.

00:59:29.840 --> 00:59:33.610
Goal number one is
focused on archiving,

00:59:33.610 --> 00:59:38.690
and is mainly a load function
in terms of a typical ETL job.

00:59:38.690 --> 00:59:41.630
Specifically, I want to
do some basic formatting

00:59:41.630 --> 00:59:43.590
on every event, and
write each event

00:59:43.590 --> 00:59:45.880
to a durable store for
additional processing

00:59:45.880 --> 00:59:47.270
or analysis.

00:59:47.270 --> 00:59:49.950
In this case, my durable
store is BigQuery.

00:59:49.950 --> 00:59:53.660
Note-- this data is assigned
to the job's global window,

00:59:53.660 --> 00:59:56.770
and does not wait for any
specific window to close.

00:59:56.770 --> 01:00:00.110
In essence, it is processed
as fast as possible.

01:00:00.110 --> 01:00:04.170
In this case, it's written to
BigQuery via its streaming API.

01:00:04.170 --> 01:00:07.500
The latency for this data is
a function of transmit time,

01:00:07.500 --> 01:00:10.200
plus Pub/Sub time,
plus data flow time,

01:00:10.200 --> 01:00:13.690
plus availability time
in the downstream store.

01:00:13.690 --> 01:00:15.930
Well, since I'm not
using windowing,

01:00:15.930 --> 01:00:18.820
this latency calculation
may not be very helpful,

01:00:18.820 --> 01:00:21.660
if you're trying to
determine data completeness.

01:00:21.660 --> 01:00:24.690
More specifically, if you're
trying to perform analysis

01:00:24.690 --> 01:00:27.900
over the raw events-- in
this case in BigQuery--

01:00:27.900 --> 01:00:30.250
your analysis may be
partially inaccurate

01:00:30.250 --> 01:00:34.340
if you're addressing event
times within the latency window.

01:00:34.340 --> 01:00:36.600
There are specific
techniques to deal

01:00:36.600 --> 01:00:39.990
with late arriving data, as
well as omitting early results,

01:00:39.990 --> 01:00:42.680
which I will address next.

01:00:42.680 --> 01:00:45.330
Goal number two is to
calculate the total number

01:00:45.330 --> 01:00:48.900
of plays per track, per
10-minute fix window.

01:00:48.900 --> 01:00:52.370
And I want to update the
output every 15 seconds.

01:00:52.370 --> 01:00:54.650
We view this output
like a meter.

01:00:54.650 --> 01:00:56.400
As time progresses,
the metric should

01:00:56.400 --> 01:00:58.700
increase, as long
as events arrive

01:00:58.700 --> 01:01:00.880
for that particular tracking.

01:01:00.880 --> 01:01:03.600
Since I have a
fixed window, I can

01:01:03.600 --> 01:01:05.260
enforce the duration
for how long

01:01:05.260 --> 01:01:06.995
I want to tolerate late data.

01:01:06.995 --> 01:01:09.890
And in this case, since I'm
doing some dashboarding,

01:01:09.890 --> 01:01:13.470
my threshold is small--
only 15 seconds.

01:01:13.470 --> 01:01:17.680
Any late data arriving outside
of the 10-minute window plus 15

01:01:17.680 --> 01:01:20.430
seconds will be ignored.

01:01:20.430 --> 01:01:23.390
Now, each key--
specifically a track ID--

01:01:23.390 --> 01:01:27.920
will have up to 40 updates
updating 15 seconds processing

01:01:27.920 --> 01:01:30.320
time over a 10-minute window.

01:01:30.320 --> 01:01:32.950
In the testify use case, there
are hundreds of thousands

01:01:32.950 --> 01:01:34.350
of tracks.

01:01:34.350 --> 01:01:37.970
To deal with this volume
of highly mutating data,

01:01:37.970 --> 01:01:42.790
I am using Cloud Bigtable
as the durable store.

01:01:42.790 --> 01:01:45.920
The latency for this metric
will be transmit time,

01:01:45.920 --> 01:01:48.990
plus Pub/Sub time,
plus 15 seconds,

01:01:48.990 --> 01:01:51.960
plus any additional lag
in closing the window,

01:01:51.960 --> 01:01:54.210
plus Bigtable write time.

01:01:54.210 --> 01:01:56.690
Now, writes the Bigtable
happen in milliseconds,

01:01:56.690 --> 01:01:59.050
which greatly reduces
the overall latency

01:01:59.050 --> 01:02:00.760
for the metric value.

01:02:00.760 --> 01:02:04.450
Goal number three is more of
a data warehousing pattern.

01:02:04.450 --> 01:02:07.700
Its purpose is to drive
a rollup aggregation.

01:02:07.700 --> 01:02:11.860
I want to calculate the total
number of plays per track,

01:02:11.860 --> 01:02:13.850
per 15-minute window.

01:02:13.850 --> 01:02:16.880
And in this case, I also want
to address any late arriving

01:02:16.880 --> 01:02:21.680
data up to five minutes
after the window closes.

01:02:21.680 --> 01:02:25.860
The latency here is transmit
time, plus Pub/Sub time,

01:02:25.860 --> 01:02:29.170
plus 15 minutes, plus any
additional lag in closing

01:02:29.170 --> 01:02:32.700
the window, plus write time.

01:02:32.700 --> 01:02:34.430
Now, there's also
the possibility

01:02:34.430 --> 01:02:36.800
that data could arrive
late, up to five

01:02:36.800 --> 01:02:39.620
minutes after the widow closes.

01:02:39.620 --> 01:02:41.840
And since I'm using
discarding windows,

01:02:41.840 --> 01:02:45.210
the new window will only
contain the late arriving data.

01:02:45.210 --> 01:02:47.870
For downstream
consumers, they can now

01:02:47.870 --> 01:02:50.500
be guaranteed with
high confidence

01:02:50.500 --> 01:02:53.690
that this aggregation is a
complete view of the event

01:02:53.690 --> 01:02:55.310
stream for that time period.

01:02:55.310 --> 01:02:58.040
And if they see
late arriving data,

01:02:58.040 --> 01:03:00.720
they can choose
how to handle it.

01:03:00.720 --> 01:03:03.240
Now that you have a better
idea of the processing

01:03:03.240 --> 01:03:06.550
goals for this pipeline and the
power of data flow's streaming

01:03:06.550 --> 01:03:09.310
processing model,
we'll look at what

01:03:09.310 --> 01:03:12.750
and how you can perform
additional analysis

01:03:12.750 --> 01:03:14.880
over the stream and
its aggregations

01:03:14.880 --> 01:03:18.410
using BigQuery and Bigtable.

01:03:18.410 --> 01:03:21.300
BigQuery is a fully
managed data warehouse

01:03:21.300 --> 01:03:25.910
that provides fast, scalable,
interactive analysis

01:03:25.910 --> 01:03:28.160
through familiar SQL syntax.

01:03:28.160 --> 01:03:32.720
Queries can address a few
bytes up to petabytes in scale,

01:03:32.720 --> 01:03:37.050
and also supports real-time
ingestion of data streams.

01:03:37.050 --> 01:03:40.420
BigQuery can ingest up to
100,000 rows per second,

01:03:40.420 --> 01:03:41.520
per table.

01:03:41.520 --> 01:03:43.110
And you can get
additional throughput

01:03:43.110 --> 01:03:44.660
with simple table charting.

01:03:44.660 --> 01:03:47.080
This data is
available in seconds,

01:03:47.080 --> 01:03:50.150
meaning a query that is
addressing at rest data

01:03:50.150 --> 01:03:53.260
can also address data
that was recently ingested

01:03:53.260 --> 01:03:55.810
into an unbounded table.

01:03:55.810 --> 01:03:58.060
The sources from this
data can come from places

01:03:58.060 --> 01:04:00.950
like Pub/Sub, Dataflow.

01:04:00.950 --> 01:04:03.860
Now, BigQuery is technically
a streaming engine,

01:04:03.860 --> 01:04:06.170
since it can deal
with unbounded data.

01:04:06.170 --> 01:04:08.040
However, it does
not have the ability

01:04:08.040 --> 01:04:10.290
to rationalize time space.

01:04:10.290 --> 01:04:13.470
And specifically, it does not
understand late arriving data,

01:04:13.470 --> 01:04:15.480
and does not provide
a triggering mechanism

01:04:15.480 --> 01:04:17.030
to handle late data.

01:04:17.030 --> 01:04:18.990
This is the role of
an upstream streaming

01:04:18.990 --> 01:04:20.920
engine like Cloud Dataflow.

01:04:20.920 --> 01:04:25.810
Now, if you have a steady handle
on end to end system latency,

01:04:25.810 --> 01:04:29.290
directly bypassing
computation in Dataflow

01:04:29.290 --> 01:04:30.930
may be a valid option.

01:04:30.930 --> 01:04:33.630
At the same time, if you are
using Dataflow to stream data

01:04:33.630 --> 01:04:36.710
into BigQuery it also
makes a lot of sense

01:04:36.710 --> 01:04:40.350
to potentially pre-aggregate
data-- for example, performing

01:04:40.350 --> 01:04:43.590
group buys and simple
aggregations that can then

01:04:43.590 --> 01:04:45.530
be used for common queries.

01:04:45.530 --> 01:04:49.430
As you will see in my demo,
using BigQuery is super simple.

01:04:49.430 --> 01:04:51.550
You don't have to
worry about indexing,

01:04:51.550 --> 01:04:53.810
or re-sharding, or
storage resizing,

01:04:53.810 --> 01:04:56.020
or adding server capacity.

01:04:56.020 --> 01:05:01.200
You simply load or stream
your data into BigQuery,

01:05:01.200 --> 01:05:03.250
and then execute a query.

01:05:03.250 --> 01:05:06.070
This is the power of managed
services on the Google Cloud

01:05:06.070 --> 01:05:07.730
platform.

01:05:07.730 --> 01:05:10.850
This is the BigQuery console
in my Google Cloud project.

01:05:10.850 --> 01:05:13.850
I have an existing data set for
my streaming workflow called

01:05:13.850 --> 01:05:14.960
Testify.

01:05:14.960 --> 01:05:18.460
This dataset contains raw events
streaming from my Dataflow

01:05:18.460 --> 01:05:22.340
pipeline, as well as other data,
like track count by window,

01:05:22.340 --> 01:05:26.120
and related data, like
artist releases and labels.

01:05:26.120 --> 01:05:29.346
Let's take a look
at the event table.

01:05:29.346 --> 01:05:32.580
The event table schema is
fairly straightforward.

01:05:32.580 --> 01:05:36.460
It has columns like event
ID, event time, user ID,

01:05:36.460 --> 01:05:39.080
track ID, and create time.

01:05:39.080 --> 01:05:41.110
The table itself
is just a little

01:05:41.110 --> 01:05:43.850
over 4.5 terabytes in size.

01:05:43.850 --> 01:05:46.840
Now, with this raw
event data, I can

01:05:46.840 --> 01:05:50.950
create some very big
and interesting queries,

01:05:50.950 --> 01:05:54.670
especially if I join that
raw event data to other data.

01:05:54.670 --> 01:05:58.220
Here, I want to calculate the
top artists by stream count.

01:05:58.220 --> 01:06:00.560
To do that, I need
to join my raw events

01:06:00.560 --> 01:06:03.990
to track information, and track
information to release credit,

01:06:03.990 --> 01:06:06.030
in order to resolve
the artist's name.

01:06:06.030 --> 01:06:10.670
And then I'll do a group by in
order to get the track count.

01:06:10.670 --> 01:06:13.900
Now, it should take
between 20 and 30 seconds

01:06:13.900 --> 01:06:16.410
for this query to run.

01:06:16.410 --> 01:06:20.780
There you go, 26 and 1/2
seconds processing just

01:06:20.780 --> 01:06:23.210
under half a terabyte of data.

01:06:23.210 --> 01:06:28.660
And sadly, Justin Bieber is the
top artist by total streams.

01:06:28.660 --> 01:06:30.160
Now, the power here
is that I didn't

01:06:30.160 --> 01:06:32.710
have to worry about
sharding or indexes.

01:06:32.710 --> 01:06:35.870
I simply wrote my
query, and executed it.

01:06:35.870 --> 01:06:39.660
And having access to my
real-time information

01:06:39.660 --> 01:06:41.310
could be very
valuable, depending

01:06:41.310 --> 01:06:43.840
on my data completeness needs.

01:06:43.840 --> 01:06:46.310
Now, let's take a
look at the output

01:06:46.310 --> 01:06:50.560
from the rollup aggregation
driven by my Dataflow job.

01:06:50.560 --> 01:06:56.430
If you recall, this was a
counts of tracks by track ID

01:06:56.430 --> 01:06:57.890
by a 15-minute window.

01:06:57.890 --> 01:07:01.740
And that transform also
handled late arriving data.

01:07:01.740 --> 01:07:06.360
So I'm going to go back and
grab the track ID from Justin

01:07:06.360 --> 01:07:11.100
Bieber, and add that as
part of my where clause,

01:07:11.100 --> 01:07:13.710
and run this query.

01:07:13.710 --> 01:07:16.770
So I'm going to filter Justin
Bieber to see what his track

01:07:16.770 --> 01:07:18.800
counts look like over time.

01:07:18.800 --> 01:07:21.020
Now, since this is
a roll up, the query

01:07:21.020 --> 01:07:24.821
ran much faster,
around 1.7 seconds.

01:07:24.821 --> 01:07:26.320
And you can see
that the track count

01:07:26.320 --> 01:07:31.700
metric is calculated by
window, that 15-minute window.

01:07:31.700 --> 01:07:34.550
And if data arrive
late, the window--

01:07:34.550 --> 01:07:37.870
specifically the timing--
would have been marked as late.

01:07:37.870 --> 01:07:39.800
You can also see
that the latency

01:07:39.800 --> 01:07:42.590
between the end of the
window and the write time

01:07:42.590 --> 01:07:45.150
is roughly around 30 seconds.

01:07:45.150 --> 01:07:48.870
So the total latency would be
the 15 minutes of the window,

01:07:48.870 --> 01:07:51.070
plus the latency
of the write time,

01:07:51.070 --> 01:07:53.860
or around 15 minutes
and 30 seconds.

01:07:53.860 --> 01:07:58.400
This is highly accurate analysis
against a real-time stream.

01:07:58.400 --> 01:07:59.710
Now, this is great.

01:07:59.710 --> 01:08:03.050
But let's explore even
lower latency output

01:08:03.050 --> 01:08:05.580
from our dashboarding transform.

01:08:05.580 --> 01:08:10.320
The output from that transform
is written to Cloud Bigtable.

01:08:10.320 --> 01:08:14.310
Cloud Bigtable is Google's
NoSQL big database service.

01:08:14.310 --> 01:08:16.510
It's managed and is
designed to handle

01:08:16.510 --> 01:08:19.470
massive loads with consistent
low latency and high

01:08:19.470 --> 01:08:20.500
throughput.

01:08:20.500 --> 01:08:23.760
It's an excellent solution
for highly mutating data

01:08:23.760 --> 01:08:26.590
over large key ranges.

01:08:26.590 --> 01:08:29.540
Here's my current
testify cluster.

01:08:29.540 --> 01:08:33.069
And, as configured,
it can handle

01:08:33.069 --> 01:08:38.864
reads and writes at 30,000 QPS
with a consistent latency of 6

01:08:38.864 --> 01:08:39.405
milliseconds.

01:08:42.689 --> 01:08:47.660
Now, the dashboarding transform
from my Dataflow pipeline emits

01:08:47.660 --> 01:08:52.970
accumulating updates every
15 seconds for a given track

01:08:52.970 --> 01:08:54.779
and window key.

01:08:54.779 --> 01:08:57.100
Let's go back to
the Dataflow console

01:08:57.100 --> 01:08:59.590
and look at the
current data watermark

01:08:59.590 --> 01:09:03.370
from my Cloud Bigtable I/O step.

01:09:03.370 --> 01:09:11.020
So right now, the data
watermark is at 21:41, basically

01:09:11.020 --> 01:09:17.550
in the 40th minute
segments in the 2100 hour.

01:09:17.550 --> 01:09:21.080
Using my HBase
clients, I'm going

01:09:21.080 --> 01:09:25.220
to call a get on tracks
tied to the track

01:09:25.220 --> 01:09:27.590
key for Justin Bieber.

01:09:27.590 --> 01:09:33.420
And I'm going to look at the
fourth hour and 40th minute

01:09:33.420 --> 01:09:35.069
window.

01:09:35.069 --> 01:09:37.580
And there's the value right now.

01:09:37.580 --> 01:09:43.430
He's had 25,147
streams in that window.

01:09:43.430 --> 01:09:47.229
Now, depending where I'm
at in that 15 second cycle,

01:09:47.229 --> 01:09:50.050
we should see that
increase, probably

01:09:50.050 --> 01:09:52.670
caught a rate on the end there.

01:09:52.670 --> 01:09:53.490
There you go.

01:09:53.490 --> 01:09:56.820
So now it's at 27,302.

01:09:56.820 --> 01:10:00.700
And this will continue to
accumulate all the way up

01:10:00.700 --> 01:10:05.370
to the four hour and
50 minute window.

01:10:05.370 --> 01:10:07.420
And then we'll cycle a new key.

01:10:07.420 --> 01:10:10.420
And the accumulator
will start over.

01:10:10.420 --> 01:10:12.340
So this is fantastic.

01:10:12.340 --> 01:10:14.330
I have thousands and
thousands of keys.

01:10:14.330 --> 01:10:17.350
And I can continue to
mutate them, the values,

01:10:17.350 --> 01:10:19.510
over these windows.

01:10:19.510 --> 01:10:22.650
Now, the access time
here is a bit inflated.

01:10:22.650 --> 01:10:25.050
So it's roughly
about 0.38 seconds,

01:10:25.050 --> 01:10:28.010
mainly because I'm querying
from my laptop into the cloud.

01:10:28.010 --> 01:10:29.850
If I was doing
this on the cloud,

01:10:29.850 --> 01:10:35.970
I'd be seeing response times
at 6, 10-ish millisecond range.

01:10:35.970 --> 01:10:38.210
The point here is
that this track count

01:10:38.210 --> 01:10:43.540
is dead accurate within 15
seconds of the live stream.

01:10:43.540 --> 01:10:47.350
Now, let's pull
this all together.

01:10:47.350 --> 01:10:51.840
I built a dashboard
in Data Studio 360.

01:10:51.840 --> 01:10:54.340
Google Data Studio
360 turns your data

01:10:54.340 --> 01:10:56.640
into beautiful,
informative reports

01:10:56.640 --> 01:11:00.500
that are easy to read
and easy to share.

01:11:00.500 --> 01:11:04.570
It seamlessly integrates with
BigQuery, Cloud SQL, MySQL,

01:11:04.570 --> 01:11:07.860
Google Analytics, AdWords,
and many other data sources.

01:11:07.860 --> 01:11:10.520
I chose it because
it's easy to use.

01:11:10.520 --> 01:11:14.390
But you could easily apply
these same processing techniques

01:11:14.390 --> 01:11:18.180
to drive homegrown
reports, or integrate

01:11:18.180 --> 01:11:22.830
the data into maybe some
existing tablo deployments.

01:11:22.830 --> 01:11:25.930
Once you have your windowing and
correctness techniques dialed

01:11:25.930 --> 01:11:30.190
in, the possibilities are
endless for highly accurate,

01:11:30.190 --> 01:11:35.000
low latency streaming data
processing and analysis.

01:11:35.000 --> 01:11:38.200
Now, as I start to wrap
up, I want to leave you

01:11:38.200 --> 01:11:39.976
with some general guidance.

01:11:39.976 --> 01:11:44.520
To start with, ingest
everything into BigQuery.

01:11:44.520 --> 01:11:46.620
Now, there are some exceptions.

01:11:46.620 --> 01:11:50.110
If you have a very
fast-changing data,

01:11:50.110 --> 01:11:53.446
Cloud Bigtable may be
a better fit for you.

01:11:53.446 --> 01:11:57.970
Cloud Bigtable is our
managed HBase implementation.

01:11:57.970 --> 01:12:01.610
It's good if you're building
a real-time leaderboard

01:12:01.610 --> 01:12:05.680
and you need to constantly
update the same keyspace.

01:12:05.680 --> 01:12:07.920
Bigtable is a much better fit.

01:12:07.920 --> 01:12:10.760
Another thing to consider
is decide if you really need

01:12:10.760 --> 01:12:12.740
to do event time processing.

01:12:12.740 --> 01:12:16.420
And what cases do you need to
cover that require event time

01:12:16.420 --> 01:12:17.430
processing?

01:12:17.430 --> 01:12:20.250
Event time processing is more
resource intensive to use.

01:12:20.250 --> 01:12:21.780
So use it wisely.

01:12:21.780 --> 01:12:24.570
I strongly suggest that you
use Google Cloud Dataflow

01:12:24.570 --> 01:12:27.060
to produce aggregations
that are going

01:12:27.060 --> 01:12:30.480
to be commonly used for
queries in another system,

01:12:30.480 --> 01:12:31.700
like BigQuery.

01:12:31.700 --> 01:12:34.600
This will ultimately reduce
the time and resource

01:12:34.600 --> 01:12:37.740
cost related to those queries.

01:12:37.740 --> 01:12:41.570
Here's some general guidance
for Dataflow as you get started.

01:12:41.570 --> 01:12:43.340
Now, the first one
challenges everything

01:12:43.340 --> 01:12:45.050
that I said about streaming.

01:12:45.050 --> 01:12:47.260
Keep in mind that
streaming isn't a solution

01:12:47.260 --> 01:12:49.110
to all problems.

01:12:49.110 --> 01:12:53.870
For example, performing a bulk
database snapshot migration

01:12:53.870 --> 01:12:56.480
is much better suited
for a batch job.

01:12:56.480 --> 01:12:58.470
And, specifically, you
could run that batch job

01:12:58.470 --> 01:13:00.340
with Cloud Dataflow.

01:13:00.340 --> 01:13:04.250
Now, the main decision point
about using batch or streaming

01:13:04.250 --> 01:13:06.970
will be around the
boundedness, and your ability

01:13:06.970 --> 01:13:09.660
to deal with latency
and data correctness.

01:13:09.660 --> 01:13:12.270
In either case of
batch or streaming,

01:13:12.270 --> 01:13:15.720
utilize auto scaling to remove
the guesswork from resource

01:13:15.720 --> 01:13:17.750
capacity planning.

01:13:17.750 --> 01:13:21.660
If you are going to use windows,
specifically session windows,

01:13:21.660 --> 01:13:22.730
use them carefully.

01:13:22.730 --> 01:13:24.170
They are very powerful.

01:13:24.170 --> 01:13:26.390
But they're also very
resource intensive.

01:13:26.390 --> 01:13:28.440
In a lot of cases, you
may be able to use fixed

01:13:28.440 --> 01:13:31.360
windows over session windows.

01:13:31.360 --> 01:13:33.400
This next one may be obvious.

01:13:33.400 --> 01:13:35.660
But the guidance
says to prune output

01:13:35.660 --> 01:13:38.050
from your transforms
inside of your pipeline

01:13:38.050 --> 01:13:39.210
as much as possible.

01:13:39.210 --> 01:13:42.900
This helps reduce network
transmission and serialization

01:13:42.900 --> 01:13:44.260
overhead.

01:13:44.260 --> 01:13:47.600
If you are using Dataflow in
streaming mode with Pub/Sub,

01:13:47.600 --> 01:13:50.790
I encourage you to use
additional subscriptions

01:13:50.790 --> 01:13:54.760
in order to do backfill
or emergency replay.

01:13:54.760 --> 01:13:57.430
And, finally, I encourage
you to use custom

01:13:57.430 --> 01:13:59.600
transforms as much as possible.

01:13:59.600 --> 01:14:01.470
This will help
with composability,

01:14:01.470 --> 01:14:04.410
as you build more
and new pipelines.

01:14:04.410 --> 01:14:07.240
For BigQuery, I have
the following guidance.

01:14:07.240 --> 01:14:09.870
I already said this,
but I'll say it again.

01:14:09.870 --> 01:14:12.560
Ingest everything into BigQuery.

01:14:12.560 --> 01:14:15.430
It's cost effective,
especially when

01:14:15.430 --> 01:14:17.420
addressing historical data.

01:14:17.420 --> 01:14:20.870
At a penny per gig, it is
an excellent alternative

01:14:20.870 --> 01:14:22.960
to standard blob storage.

01:14:22.960 --> 01:14:28.710
Plus, you have access to it via
a query, or via Cloud Dataflow.

01:14:28.710 --> 01:14:31.850
Next guidance is, select only
the columns that you need.

01:14:31.850 --> 01:14:34.470
Again, sounds obvious,
but I've seen query

01:14:34.470 --> 01:14:36.820
after query that
wastefully addresses

01:14:36.820 --> 01:14:39.130
data that is never used.

01:14:39.130 --> 01:14:43.570
Next, consider restricting
access to raw event tables,

01:14:43.570 --> 01:14:45.760
especially if you're
following this guidance

01:14:45.760 --> 01:14:49.910
around streaming and streaming
everything into BigQuery.

01:14:49.910 --> 01:14:52.670
And you restrict
access to a few folks

01:14:52.670 --> 01:14:55.515
whose job it is to write queries
to answer business questions.

01:14:55.515 --> 01:14:59.600
Now, unfettered access
to all data is great.

01:14:59.600 --> 01:15:02.340
But it comes with some
risks, both security concerns

01:15:02.340 --> 01:15:04.050
as well as cost control.

01:15:04.050 --> 01:15:06.740
Fortunately, BigQuery
has a very flexible model

01:15:06.740 --> 01:15:09.590
to control table access.

01:15:09.590 --> 01:15:13.430
And, finally, if you need
more processing power,

01:15:13.430 --> 01:15:15.400
simply request more slots.

01:15:15.400 --> 01:15:18.070
We're here to serve.

01:15:18.070 --> 01:15:21.480
Streaming data processing,
when implemented correctly,

01:15:21.480 --> 01:15:24.060
can reduce the time
to answer and increase

01:15:24.060 --> 01:15:25.920
accuracy of analysis.

01:15:25.920 --> 01:15:29.830
Event time processing is a
critical architectural need

01:15:29.830 --> 01:15:33.210
when attempting to
rationalize data completeness.

01:15:33.210 --> 01:15:36.850
Now, some streaming engines
are more robust than others.

01:15:36.850 --> 01:15:38.640
And this is a good thing.

01:15:38.640 --> 01:15:42.330
It all comes down to the
ability to couple these systems

01:15:42.330 --> 01:15:46.050
together, and make sure that
they work better together.

01:15:46.050 --> 01:15:49.000
Managed services
remove the element

01:15:49.000 --> 01:15:51.990
of human interaction
and human costs,

01:15:51.990 --> 01:15:56.640
specifically deploying and
babysitting infrastructure.

01:15:56.640 --> 01:16:00.900
Data processing analysis is
about writing amazing pipelines

01:16:00.900 --> 01:16:03.220
and queries to solve
business problems,

01:16:03.220 --> 01:16:05.710
not deploying and
managing servers.

01:16:05.710 --> 01:16:09.160
Now, I only touched on Cloud
Pub/Sub, Cloud Dataflow,

01:16:09.160 --> 01:16:11.290
and BigQuery really
to drill home

01:16:11.290 --> 01:16:15.300
the point about the power of
managed services and streaming

01:16:15.300 --> 01:16:17.290
engine composability.

01:16:17.290 --> 01:16:20.400
But there is so much additional
power on the Google Cloud

01:16:20.400 --> 01:16:22.870
platform for data processing.

01:16:22.870 --> 01:16:25.390
If you want to bring your
existing Hadoop workloads

01:16:25.390 --> 01:16:29.560
to the Cloud, great, we
have amazing managed support

01:16:29.560 --> 01:16:32.450
for Hadoop via Cloud Dataproc.

01:16:32.450 --> 01:16:36.460
If you want ultra low latency,
high throughput, durable

01:16:36.460 --> 01:16:40.660
storage for changing
key data, Cloud Bigtable

01:16:40.660 --> 01:16:42.710
is an excellent
managed alternative

01:16:42.710 --> 01:16:45.050
to standalone HBase.

01:16:45.050 --> 01:16:47.650
If you want highly
available, globally

01:16:47.650 --> 01:16:52.620
addressable NoSQL Datastore
is an excellent solution.

01:16:52.620 --> 01:16:57.430
If you want managed
MySQL, try out Cloud SQL.

01:16:57.430 --> 01:17:00.250
Now, beyond being a platform
leader for streaming

01:17:00.250 --> 01:17:03.420
processing, the
Google Cloud platform

01:17:03.420 --> 01:17:07.150
is an amazing place for any
scale of data processing

01:17:07.150 --> 01:17:09.020
and analysis.

01:17:09.020 --> 01:17:11.940
I will leave you with
the simplest of advice.

01:17:11.940 --> 01:17:14.200
If you have not
already, I encourage

01:17:14.200 --> 01:17:18.150
you to go and read the docs
for Cloud Pub/Sub, Dataflow,

01:17:18.150 --> 01:17:19.200
and BigQuery.

01:17:19.200 --> 01:17:23.160
Understand the value and
feature set of each product.

01:17:23.160 --> 01:17:26.050
Then, specifically, go
get out the Cloud Dataflow

01:17:26.050 --> 01:17:27.630
mobile gaming example.

01:17:27.630 --> 01:17:31.100
It's a solid implementation
of a feature set, grounded

01:17:31.100 --> 01:17:33.540
in real world use cases.

01:17:33.540 --> 01:17:35.770
Then write your own pipeline.

01:17:35.770 --> 01:17:37.400
But keep it simple.

01:17:37.400 --> 01:17:41.270
Answer one question, end to
end, and then build from there.

01:17:41.270 --> 01:17:43.380
Finally, have fun.

01:17:43.380 --> 01:17:44.200
Thank you.

01:17:44.200 --> 01:17:46.650
[MUSIC PLAYING]

